{"id":"one-line-3","title":"Every organization on one line: positions 101 to 150","question":"For the 150 organizations the City pays most, what do the two sets of books show side by side, and how many of the eight tests does each trip?","finding":"The 150 received $10.44 billion from the City over fiscal 2019 to 2026. 79 trip none of the eight tests, 42 trip one, 20 trip two and 9 trip three or more; the most is 4, at Hyde Street Community Services Inc. The tests tripped most often among them are reserves (22), receivables or other lines (21), filings (16). 2 of the 150 have no return on file to test. This page runs from Edgewood Center For Children And Familes (0 tests) to Kimochi Inc (0).","how":"The 150 organizations paid most by the City over fiscal 2019 to 2026, among those tied to a federal tax number. City totals are from the Controller's table of every payment, less loans, district assessments handed over, benefit-plan trust payments and debt service, and include aid that passes through an organization to others. Figures are from the latest full-year, full-form return; City paid in that tax year uses the alignment rule of the first screen. A test is tripped when the organization appears in the full list behind the matching screen or screens: (1) City paid above revenue or spending, unless its own balance sheet covers the gap; (2) net assets unexplained, or closing balances not carried forward; (3) reserves; (4) programme share; (5) payroll, except a low cost per employee alone; (6) a contractor taking a quarter of spending or sharing its address; (7) a paid year with no return, a return more than a year late, an amended return that moved the figures, a change of accounting method, or months no return covers; (8) a receivable that disappeared without being collected or keeps growing, or a large catch-all line with no description, or other changes in net assets or a prior-period adjustment worth a quarter of spending. The government-grants screen and plain changes of financial year do not count. Sorted by tests tripped, then by City money; the table is cut into three pages of 50.","limits":"A count of tests is a reading aid, not a score of conduct: the tests are of unequal weight, several fire on form-filling habits, and large, complicated organizations have more lines on which to trip. An organization with no tests tripped has not been cleared of anything; one with several has not been accused of anything. 4 payee names paid enough to rank here could not be tied to a tax number and are absent: Aids Housing Alliance SF ($20.4 million), Portola Family Connections Inc ($17.7 million), Civic ($12.7 million), Asian Women's Shelter ($12.5 million). City money is taken from the Controller's table of every payment, not from its narrower nonprofit-spending table, which keeps contracted services only. Left out of it, each picked out by the payment file's own label, are: loans; money the City collects for a benefit district and hands over (the district's own assessments); benefit-plan payments from a trust fund; and debt service. For 23 of the 485 organizations matched here that makes the City figure at least $1 million and 10% higher over fiscal 2019 to 2026, $1.36 billion higher in all. The figure keeps grants and contracts the nonprofit-spending table lacks, emergency purchases, and aid paid through an organization (day care assistance, rent assistance paid on behalf of clients, in-home care contract payments). Part of that aid passes on to others, so it is a real City payment but not all of it is payment for the organization's own work. A return may not count money that passes through as revenue. For the organizations matched here, that leaves out $78.8 million of loans (9 organizations), $425.1 million of money the City collected for a benefit district and handed over (14 organizations) and $495.1 million of benefit-plan payments from a trust fund (1 organization), fiscal 2019 to 2026. District assessments are covered on the screen “Benefit districts: assessments in, City grants on top”, which keeps assessments and City grants apart. The fiscal year is the one printed on each payment, which can be later than the year the payment was made. Organizations are matched to tax returns by payee name and employer number; a wrong match would create a false gap. A pattern here is a reason to ask for the audited statements, not a finding.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"rank","t":"Position","type":"int"},{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"tests","t":"Tests tripped","type":"int"},{"k":"which","t":"Which","type":"text"},{"k":"cityTotal","t":"City paid, fiscal 2019 to 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"year","t":"Latest full return, year ended","type":"date"},{"k":"cityPaid","t":"City paid in that tax year","type":"usd"},{"k":"revenue","t":"Revenue","type":"usd"},{"k":"expenses","t":"Expenses","type":"usd"},{"k":"surplus","t":"Surplus or deficit","type":"usd"},{"k":"months","t":"Months of spending in reserve","type":"int"},{"k":"programShare","t":"Programme share of spending","type":"pct"},{"k":"cityShare","t":"City money as share of revenue","type":"pct"},{"k":"ret","t":"Source","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"org":"Edgewood Center For Children And Familes","orgKey":"EDGEWOOD CENTER CHILDREN FAMILES","year":"2025-06-30","ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/941186168/202631349349308578/full"},"cityTotal":75948231,"tests":0,"which":"None","cityPaid":9851928,"revenue":32169637,"expenses":35088516,"surplus":-2918879,"months":9.6,"programShare":0.806,"cityShare":0.306,"rank":101},{"org":"Carecen","orgKey":"CARECEN","year":"2024-12-31","ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/943036508/202542889349302429/full"},"cityTotal":61417681,"tests":0,"which":"None","cityPaid":8380609,"revenue":10597791,"expenses":10693929,"surplus":-96138,"months":0.7,"programShare":0.892,"cityShare":0.791,"rank":102},{"org":"Homeless Childre